  "body": {
    "id": "thm:global-endpoint",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "If \\(\\Delta_j\\ge0\\) for every active \\(j=(C,s)\\in J\\), then \\(\\rho^{\\star}=1-\\rho_0\\) is the unique maximizing holdout fraction for the full-cell conditional-power objective. At that fraction every binding cell has an optimal weight in \\([\\theta_{0j},\\pi/2)\\), with equality to \\(\\theta_{0j}\\) exactly when \\(\\Delta_j=0\\); slack-cell weights are the full superlevel sets required by \\(\\mathcal{S}^{\\star}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "C",
      "S",
      "EC",
      "Delta",
      "J",
      "rho",
      "rho0",
      "theta",
      "Sstar"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:positive-compact-alternatives",
      "thm:least-coordinate-reduction",
      "thm:weight-side",
      "lem:realizability-bound",
      "def:design-objective"
    ],
    "justification": "A primitive sign certificate gives the unique largest-holdout decision for any finite collection of active cells.",
    "gap": "No comparator derives this finite-population-realizable endpoint certificate.",
    "consumer": "A design with all nonnegative discrepancies avoids global numerical holdout search.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(j\\) with \\(\\Delta_j\\ge0\\) and write \\(P(\\rho,\\theta)=\\Pi_j(\\rho,\\theta,\\ell_s)\\).  In the notation of the weight-side proof, differentiation gives, for \\(r>0\\),\n\\[\nP_a=h\\mu(L)\\left[Q\\!\\left(\\frac{q-dh-rL}{v}\\right)-P\\right]<0,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sign}(P_r)=\\operatorname{sign}(rd-a).\n\\]\nThe strict inequality holds because conditional rejection probability increases strictly with the selected Gaussian coordinate, so its boundary value is below its selected-tail average.  Every individually optimal angle obeys \\(\\theta\\ge\\theta_0\\), and on this interval \\(\\cos\\theta\\,d\\le b_1\\).  Hence\n\\[\na-rd=t\\{A-\\kappa\\cos\\theta\\,d\\}\\ge t\\Delta\\ge0,\n\\]\nso \\(P_r\\le0\\).  Since \\(a_\\rho=-A/(2t)\\) and \\(r_\\rho=-\\kappa\\cos\\theta/(2t)\\),\n\\[\nP_\\rho=-\\frac{AP_a+\\kappa\\cos\\theta P_r}{2t}>0\n\\]\nat every optimal non-pure angle.  At \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\), power is the constant \\(p_2=Q\\{\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha)-b_2\\ell_s\\}\\).\n\nAt \\(\\rho=1-\\rho_0\\), the inward angular derivative from \\(\\pi/2\\) is\n\\[\n\\phi\\{\\Phi^{-1}(1-\\alpha)-b_2h\\}\n\\left[b_1h-\\kappa t\\{\\mu(c)-\\mu(c-Ath)\\}\\right].\n\\]\nBecause \\(0<\\mu'<1\\), its bracket exceeds \\(h(b_1-\\kappa At^2)\\).  The realizability bound and \\(t^2=\\rho_0<1/2\\) yield\n\\[\n\\frac{\\kappa At^2}{b_1}\n\\le\\rho_0\\frac{5\\sqrt{15}}{18}<1,\n\\]\nso the pure-stage-two value is strictly improvable at the endpoint.\n\nThus \\(M_j(\\rho)\\) is strictly smaller at every \\(\\rho<1-\\rho_0\\): if its optimizer is pure stage two, its value is \\(p_2\\), below the endpoint value; otherwise its fixed-angle power strictly increases toward the endpoint.  Choose a cell binding in the finite minimum at the endpoint.  Its strict inequality implies\n\\[\nF(\\rho)<F(1-\\rho_0)\\qquad(\\rho<1-\\rho_0),\n\\]\nproving uniqueness of the holdout fraction.  The weight-side theorem gives the binding-cell angle location and equality stratum, while the definition of \\(\\mathcal S^\\star\\) gives the stated superlevel sets for slack cells.",
